qcom_rtc: the PMIC RTC's alarm as a wake-up; power_wakeup

The alarm peripheral (the node's second reg, 0x6100 on the PM6125; Linux's
rtc-pm8xxx pm8941_regs: ALARM_RW 0x40, ALARM_CTRL 0x46 bit 7, ALARM_CTRL2
0x48 bit 0) with its interrupt through the SPMI arbiter.
dev.qcom_rtc.0.wakealarm is Linux's /sys/class/rtc/rtc0/wakealarm: the
alarm's real time (UTC seconds), 0 off; converted with the difference
between the system clock and the count at that moment, since the count
cannot be set (as Leste's librtc-realtime does for alarmd). The alarm's
interrupt switches it off, counts it (dev.qcom_rtc.0.alarms), tells devd
(PMIC/RTC/ALARM) and raises power_wakeup (qcom_spmi.h), which the power
key's press raises too: a suspend in progress ends (freebsd/pm's
pro1x_pm). The registers' sequences in freebsd/pm's
sys/dev/qcom_spmi/qcom_rtc_alarm.h (host test: pm/test/qcom_rtc_test.c).
hw.qcom_rtc.alarm_clear=1: the status cleared as qpnp-rtc does (CTRL2's bit
written 1), else as Linux 7.1.

files.arm64: qcom_rtc_sim, QEMU's stand-in (pm/test/qemu's PRO1XQ):
qcom_rtc.c on QEMU virt's PL031.

